hi there steve kaufman  this is going to be the first in a  series of seven videos where i deal with  each of the seven habits of highly  effective people that stephen covey uh  covers in his book and which i made  reference to in my video last week  uh the first well first of all i'll go  over very quickly the seven habits and  this is advice for people in their lives  advice for managers advice for business  people and and for people generally and  the stephen covey and you can google  stephen covey c-o-v-e-y  seven habits and you'll get a lot more  information but there are  seven habits the first three  uh have to do with independence the  second three have to do with  interdependence and the seventh one has  to do with constantly renewing yourself  and constantly improving so the first  one today i'm going to talk about is the  need to be proactive  i think it's true in many things in life  if we wait for things to happen to us  some things may have i mean many things  will happen to you  however by being proactive by taking  initiatives  by pursuing your goals uh it's more  likely that good things are going to  happen to you and certainly  i found in language learning that as  long as i was a passive learner in  school  uh with the teacher teaching me and as  long as i waited for the teacher somehow  or the textbook to teach me i wasn't  making very good progress  once i took control of my learning once  i went out and and this means looking  for  things in the language that interest me  books uh  in the case of chinese books with in  those days before the the internet books  with um vocabulary lists behind each  chapter  taking the initiative initiative in  terms of what i want to learn about what  aspects of the language i need to work  on uh taking the initiative  not to stay with the program  that the teacher is is  leading you  into in the classroom  many teachers don't like this they don't  want the students to get ahead of them  this is so wrong because  the language  isn't just chapter one chapter two  chapter three the language is is  basically a whole  package and we learn  we learn different parts of it again and  again and again and so for the student  to go out and find things of interest to  him or her and then go back and do what  they're doing in class all of this is  good you have to take the initiative you  have to take responsibility there are  people who say  that there's no bad learner there's only  a bad teacher i don't believe that at  all i think whether you're in a  classroom or whether you are studying on  your own  only those learners who take the  initiative who are motivated enough to  take charge of their learning are going  to be successful and  we have ample evidence of this in our  school system in canada and i'm sure in  other language schools where a small  percentage of the learners actually  improve  and  an equally large number can improve on  their own if they're motivated so that  the  important point is not the number of  instructional hours the important is  point is to what extent are you prepared  to be proactive and take charge of your  own learning so that's the first of the  seven habits that stephen covey talks  about and it might even be the most  important  be proactive take charge of your own  learning and that's the first of the  seven habits of highly effective people  thank you for listening bye for now
 

